            "text": "{\n  \"relation\": \"REFINED\",\n  \"consensus_summary\": \"Milk symbolizes basic or elementary spiritual teachings suitable for new or spiritually immature believers, typically contrasted with the 'solid food' or 'meat' of advanced doctrine.\",\n  \"book_summary\": \"Milk represents the raw, uninterpreted word of God, which is proper for spiritual babes but meant to be 'churned' (worked and tested) to produce butter, which constitutes mature meat.\",\n  \"consensus_core_identification\": \"Elementary spiritual teachings and basic spiritual nourishment.\",\n  \"book_core_identification\": \"The word of God received prior to interpretation.\",\n  \"core_relation\": \"BOOK_NARROWS\",\n  \"extension_relation\": \"PARTIAL_RECLASSIFICATION\",\n  \"boundary_disagreement\": \"The consensus views milk and meat as distinct categories of teaching (simple vs. complex topics). The book reclassifies them as the same substance in different states: milk is the raw word, and meat (butter) is that same word after being worked or interpreted.\",\n  \"material_contradiction\": \"There is no direct contradiction regarding the target audience, as both agree milk is for spiritual babes. However, the book introduces a mechanical relationship between milk and meat (churning milk produces butter/meat) that replaces the consensus paradigm of simply moving on to deeper topics.\",\n  \"rationale\": \"The book accepts the consensus baseline that milk is foundational nourishment for the immature, but materially refines the mechanism. By synthesizing Proverbs 30:33 and Isaiah 7:15, the book argues that milk is the uninterpreted word, which must be 'churned' through testing and interpretation to yield mature food.\",\n  \"citation_support\": \"STRONG\",\n  \"strongest_objection\": \"Equating 'butter' with the 'strong meat' of Hebrews 5 requires a highly synthetic reading of Proverbs 30:33 and Isaiah 7:15 that is not explicitly present in the New Testament texts contrasting milk and meat.\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high\"\n}",
